tests:
  unit: { count: 438, covers: "Both halves of the epic, at the level where each one's rule lives. THE CORE: reach as a choice Config carries rather than a constant either server hard-codes, the arm/disarm state machine with the two properties W1's review rounds forced into it - a failed re-arm keeps the session it was replacing, and the arm lock covers the swap without ever covering the outgoing session's drain - and the addresses the control API reports for a second device to use. src/lib.rs holds the composition, src/api/* the surface, and xtask/src/mr.rs the signature change W2 was forced to make when mr confirm demanded a review of a diff that changed no code. THE SHELL AND THE SCREEN: the platform axis as the only axis, over all eight platforms rather than spot-checked - which is what makes the copy assertions worth anything, because the seven machine words are asserted ABSENT across the whole axis and not in the two places a reviewer would look. setup.ts carries the per-platform routes, verdicts.ts the state vocabulary the screen reads and never writes, proxyTargets.ts the quit dialog's clients that moved out of the way so setup could be organised by platform. The registry entries carry their own contracts: TaskStep (a number is a reading order, a state is a reading, and the state hook is absent where nothing observed one), CommandBlock, Select at a host-given width, StatusLine, Notice. DEC-13, DEC-33 and DEC-55 are each re-asserted here rather than assumed: a task's state comes from a probe, the traffic layer or the reach this app holds, never from a click, and no act self-reports a tick. 1,138 Vitest tests in 55 files and 754 in the core workspace (313 trawler, 441 xtask), all green at the tip, with eslint, tsc and the closed-vocabulary rule clean at 0 live design-allow suppressions." }
  integration: { count: 120, covers: "Each bounded contract through the real thing, on ephemeral ports and never the public network. tests/reach.rs drives the seam DEC-55 created: arming binds a second pair of listeners on one named interface BESIDE the loopback pair and never in place of it - the property the whole decision rests on, because the app's own client may not lose its route to a disarm - GET/POST/DELETE /reach round-trip, a refused arm leaves the previous state standing, and the pair dies with the process because nothing persists it. tests/ca.rs proves /ca.crt is fetchable over the reach address, which is the only thing that makes a phone's trust step possible at all. tests/api.rs carries the control surface the screen reads, including the interface addresses reported for a second device. xtask/tests/mr.rs pins the gate-tool change W2 landed: mr confirm demands a cr note only where the diff changes a code path, which is what the gate itself already did - the two now agree, asserted over real git repositories rather than mocked." }
  e2e: { count: 37, covers: "THE EPIC'S FULL FLOW, driving the real Tauri window against a real ephemeral core over a local HTTPS origin: 37 tests green across 3 spec files - 8 onboarding, 3 capture, 26 flows. onboarding.spec.ts was rewritten rather than extended at W6, on the axis the epic rebuilt the screen around. Journey 1, this machine: a fresh launch leads with what the screen is and not with the list, the picker already answers This machine - macOS, three numbered tasks read at once with the two probed ones carrying a state and the third reading 'waiting for the first request', nothing dimmed; then one proxied HTTPS request decrypted by the core ends setup - the lead is gone, a flow row names localhost, and the band carries 'set up another device'. That is the epic's promise end to end: a first-time user reaches capture. Journey 2, an off-machine platform, run BEFORE the reveal because capture latches everProven: choosing iPhone makes reach task 1 and pushes the rest down, the three dependants carry data-dim=true - dimmed rather than given steps that cannot work, and still numbered - no probe row and no device address; then answering the picker again restores this machine's three tasks, which proves the axis is reversible and no unit test of either side observes it. Plus the no-wizard case: no prev/next pair and no step-body, because the accordion retired with the stepper. The 8 assertions the old spine held are all dispositioned on the record - 5 succeeded, 1 kept verbatim, 2 retired with the accordion and with their reason. WHAT IT DOES NOT DRIVE, stated rather than left to be found: arming reach is not reachable from a spec, because confirm_reach opens a native dialog WebDriver cannot address from inside the WKWebView, App re-reads reach only from the two calls that change it, and the suite runs one window with no second launch that could come up already armed. Stubbing the confirm under the e2e feature was refused - it would be the first divergence between the e2e shell and the shipped one, and proving arming by removing the authorization DEC-55 exists to require would prove the wrong thing. The armed rendering is asserted at integration and unit instead." }
